L-R: One of the hundreds of characters in the 2013 video game Papers, Please; game developer Lucas Pope, standing in his hometown of Saitama, Japan; the captain of a cursed pirate ship fom Pope's 2018 game Return of the Obra Dinn.

Credit:

Sam Machkovech / Aurich Lawson

L-R: One of the hundreds of characters in the 2013 video game Papers, Please; game developer Lucas Pope, standing in his hometown of Saitama, Japan; the captain of a cursed pirate ship fom Pope's 2018 game Return of the Obra Dinn.

Credit:

Sam Machkovech / Aurich Lawson

SAITAMA, Japan—Return of the Obra Dinn, the latest video game from designer, programmer, artist, writer, and musician Lucas Pope, revolves around a massive cast of characters. Of course, there is one minor detail—they're all presumed dead.

In the game, players must sort through the histories and fates of dozens of men, women, and children by working as an insurance adjuster. There's a cursed cargo ship and magic, yes, but also a giant log book, a glossary, and a massive list of names to account for and cross-reference.

If you didn't know Pope's pedigree—as one of the best independent game makers in the world, and the one-man shop responsible for Ars Technica's 2013 Game of the YearPapers, Please—you might think that premise sounds humdrum. But as in his other games, Pope somehow turns the humdrum into something incredible. Upon first boot, the goal can feel intimidating. Every crew member fills the pages of your virtual book, and the task of keeping them straight is enough to set an anxious player on edge.

I think about this—the virtual book, the names, the number of souls crowded onto this cursed boat called Obra Dinn—when I enter Omiya Station, 40 minutes north of Tokyo via train, one breezy October day. Pope has lived in Japan for nearly a decade, following another decade-plus of work in the American games industry. It's here in the town of Saitama that he worked on the majority of his 2018 game, another Top 10 entry in the Ars Technica Games of the Year list.

While waiting for Pope to arrive and chat about this game and others in his career (particularly Uncharted 1 & 2), I can't help but notice how insane this station is. Pope would later describe Saitama as a lower-key alternative to the megapolis of Tokyo, but I'm taken aback by the fact that hundreds of people pass me every minute. A few dozen dead souls, recovered from a fictional pirate ship, feels quaint in comparison.

After considering his game against the relief of his home nation, I get it. Obra Dinn was Pope's chance to do some compelling things with the classic-gaming backbone of solving a mystery and juggling clues, but it is also his most intimate game: one in which the names that whisk by in a ledger might stand out for long enough to pick through, care about, and eulogize. That, in part, explains why it took him a whopping 4.5 years to finish. As I came to learn, the modern classic only happened after Pope had mastered enough game development tools (including many he'd built from scratch) to tell his most singular story yet within a video game.

To stamp or not to stamp, the basic premise of Papers, Please is so procedural.

To stamp or not to stamp, the basic premise of Papers, Please is so procedural.

Yet remedial tasks and tools can yield great power.

Yet remedial tasks and tools can yield great power.

To stamp or not to stamp, the basic premise of Papers, Please is so procedural.

Yet remedial tasks and tools can yield great power.

"The rigmarole of the procedure"

Eventually, Pope emerges at the station. He's easy to find, towering over most of the Omiya crowd at a height well over six feet, with the kind of sharp, Scandinavian features, thin goatee, and long, tousled hair that would look appropriate either on a software engineer or a heavy metal drummer. (Pope eventually admits that he also has experience with the latter.)

He leads me to a nearby Doutor Coffee location, and for good reason. Pope hasn't slept much as of late, he says, owing to Obra Dinn's impending launch the following week in October. A 5pm coffee is his ideal first stop.

Pope is careful not to let too much slip about release-week anxiety or bug-hunting trouble, perhaps to downplay how big a deal it is that he has snuck out for a coffee and chat. (I may have lucked out due to a peculiar coincidence: Obra Dinn was launching while I was in Japan for a vacation. And I'm the kind of crazy person who thinks chatting with a game-design genius is a fine way to spend my time off.)

In our hours of chatting over pastries, drinks, and noodles, one through-line becomes apparent: Pope's money-where-his-mouth-is approach to the design, production, and creation of video games. I later realize that one of his throwaway quotes fits him as a statement of intent:

"I love tools. I love making tools. And nobody likes tools."

Pope alleges that getting hired at established game studios—namely, Realtime Associates in 2003 and Naughty Dog in 2007—stemmed from his passion for developing custom toolsets for game design (meaning, outside the "bigger" tool realm of Unity, Maya, Photoshop, et al). Pope offers a list of "lesser" game-development needs: "To get scripts into the game. Get localizations into the game. Tools to place objects, to place [metadata] tags.

"Making that, it's a derivative of the actual game," Pope continues. "Some game programmers don't like to work on that, [the parts that] players don't see in the end game. But for me, that kind of work involves user interface, design, programming, and some art—all the things I love. I love making a tool that solves a really difficult task needed to get something into your game."

Pope offers an example at a hypothetical game studio: a complicated, unsexy task comes up for a single staffer, and the company's response is to tell said staffer, whose "time is cheap," to figure it out him or herself. "My thinking is, write a tool that makes it super easy, so anybody can do it. Then they actually enjoy doing it. The quality of the stuff they produce that gets into the game is way better, because you're not wasting your time with bullshit, rote things that the computer can do well. Computers are great at that sort of thing! The fact that I'm interested in that means that I'm already different from most programmers in the game industry."

I inadvertently laugh. That sounds like a review of Pope's video games, doesn't it? He nods affirmatively. "Papers, Please is the rigmarole of the procedure of doing a thing—of, 'how can I make that efficient?'"

He's right. Papers, Please revolves around the sorting and cross-referencing of data—only, well, abstracted into the identities and histories of human beings. That game assigns you the task of working as a border patrol agent, and you must play what amounts to a high-stakes game of Minesweeper, of juggling documents and verifying their text and image details. In other words: it's something that sounds like a behind-the-scenes computer tool, used to make game design more efficient, becoming an addictive, drama-filled video game.

Obra Dinn has a similar quality, in that it turns a dry-sounding idea (manage and sort a web of AI personalities to identify their identities and relationships) into a compelling interactive experience (go back in time and use magic to solve a bunch of murders).

From Malice to Gearhead

This particular quality can be found in Pope's very first game to be sold at brick-and-mortar stores: Gearhead Garage, published by Activision's "Value" division in 1999.

Gearhead asked players to examine broken cars, diagnose their problems, figure out a solution (repairing existing parts, ordering new ones), click a few times to swap parts, and declare a car fixed. Like in later Pope games, Gearhead begins in patently simple fashion, but this game begins revealing more and more complexity as players dig in.

Also like some of Pope's later, acclaimed games, Gearhead is divorced from the kind of action you might expect from a car game. Meaning: you never drive the cars.

Gearhead was developed by Pope's first company, a studio called Ratloop Games that he founded with friends he'd made online while attending Virginia Tech. The team came together from all over the world via their love of the game Quake, whether finding each other in online sessions or on forums. They began working together from their respective home countries on a "total conversion" mod of the game, dubbed Malice.

The commercial mod was so well received that it was eventually packed into a Quake retail package by the id Software game's American publisher, GT Interactive. Pope's duties on Malice included a mix of art, modeling, and back-end work. The team dubbed itself Epochalypse at the time, but they eventually changed the name to the somewhat more palatable Ratloop Games. Soon they formally incorporated in Virginia.

Unfortunately after all that coordinating, the team's first stab at a wholly original game, a "sci-fi, third-person, action-adventure game," went nowhere. This was an era of publishers dominating PC and console game shelves, and boutique retailers like Software Etc. and Babbage's were fading out. Pope's young team struggled to pitch its idea effectively, but Ratloop did notice an exploding trend at big-box retailers: racks of discount CD-ROM software sold quite well.

Thus, Pope focused on a unique idea at the time—a game in which you fix cars but never drive them. "I grew up working on cars with my dad," Pope explains. That specific experience, of tinkering with cars before he was old enough to legally drive them, stood out. "That angle worked out really well, actually," he says. "It's like a position in your mind, of what you're getting. You're getting a car mechanic game."

Ratloop Games developed and shopped the prototype to a few publishers, noticing that every major gaming company had a "value division to sell games for cheap at Wal-mart." Activision Value, an Activision subsidiary, bit on the pitch, published the game, and sold "a couple hundred thousand copies [of Gearhead], maybe." And in a rarity for the era, Ratloop's contract meant the studio retained the game's IP.

Once the game had taken off, Activision Value approached Ratloop with designs on slapping the game's concept onto other properties, such as Jet Ski Garage, but Activision wanted to bolt a racing game on top of that system. At the time, Pope wasn't interested in making a sequel that offered anything beyond fixing a vehicle and parking it in a virtual garage. This stalemate, plus a lack of other projects and some Ratloop staffers' expiring visas, led to the development team "petering out" and its members going their separate ways.

The DS taketh, and the PSP giveth back

Years later, Pope admits that he tried to make a Gearhead sequel for the Game Boy Advance, and his unfinished prototype went so far as to bolt an "adventure" mode onto the experience. Players would actually drive the cars they had built. "I had wanted to work on consoles, and specifically Game Boy," Pope says, so he put legwork into getting serviceable 3D elements working in the game. The appeal of the original Gearhead, he points out, was that it allowed players to manipulate 3D car parts back when such 3D stuff was still a novelty on PCs. He wanted to cash in on a similar 3D-novelty with the weak GBA hardware.

"But then the DS came out," Pope adds. His project's 3D novelty on a weaker system stopped being a selling point. Gearhead Advance's potential as a product had run out, he believed, so the developer said to himself, "I'm gonna get a real job." (Pope eventually uploaded the unfinished game's ROM to the Internet, playable in emulators and on GBA flash carts.)

Pope soon moved to Los Angeles, only to find himself quickly landing a role at a game studio: Realtime Associates, a game developer with a "serious games" portfolio funded in part by Pam Omidyar. (If that name sounds familiar, it is because she's the wife of original eBay founder Pierre Omidyar.)

"Games really suck until the last two or three months."

Pope's biggest project at Realtime was a 2006 PC shooter called Re-mission, which Pope described as a serious-games take on Fantastic Voyage. The game's purpose was to "teach kids to take their chemo, because chemo can make [cancer patients] really sick." Pope admits being particularly proud of that game, which kids were exposed to by way of console-ized PCs being set up in medical centers. "We did that sort of thing, and it worked!" he exclaims.

Once Re-mission's development was complete, a mix of factors led Pope away from Realtime. For one, its next project revolved around a "military contract," which he wasn't interested in participating in. For another, Realtime's shift to serious games (and away from cartoony platformers for the Sega Saturn like Bug and Bug Too) meant a "tense" relationship between the dev team and its investors. "The thing about games is, games really suck until the last two or three months," Pope says. Realtime's investors, coming from outside the game-development world, didn't see eye to eye on that point while waiting for projects to complete.

Pope's attention was also pulled in Naughty Dog's direction once he saw a target render of how the first Uncharted game was meant to look. "That's fucking awesome," Pope thought at the time. "That's like Indiana Jones, exactly what I want to work on." Having tried and failed to land a gig at Looking Glass Studios during the development of Thief: The Dark Project, Pope fulfilled a promise to himself: that he would not slouch on the next possible game-studio gig that got him that excited ever again.

Pope points to a bit of bittersweet serendipity in play, as well: a Jak and Daxter project for Sony's PSP had been canceled, and upon its cancellation, Naughty Dog went on a hiring spree. "It's crazy to think: when I got the job there, there was no full-time GUI tools guy at all," Pope says. "Just command-line, back-end tools people." (Worth noting: Naughty Dog has let slip details about a canceled Jak and Daxter game, but this is the first we've heard about any such project for Sony's first portable.)

Naughty Dog: Saving the day, reacting to Gears

At Naughty Dog, Pope hit the ground running, arriving roughly eight months before the game shipped. He built no less than four major tools for the rest of the Naughty Dog design and art squads: a GUI tool "for the designers to map out all the levels," dubbed Charter; the game's menus; the game's save system; and a localization toolset to manage "thousands and thousands" of voice and text files for roughly 12 languages. "Previously, [localization] was just like one person with an Excel spreadsheet, doing it all herself," Pope says.

Former Naughty Dog President Christophe Balestra minced no words when telling Ars about this period over the phone: "Lucas joined at a time when we had a big crisis with our [Uncharted] tool pipeline," he said. "We were desperate to find a good tools programmer. He was part of the people who saved the day."

Pope's comfort level with a mix of programming, design, and art paid off with a task like building a menu system. He had a comfort with designing icons, placing them, choosing fonts, and making every single menu page, tree, and toggle flow into and out of each other. Instead of having each step of the process require a dedicated artist or programmer, "I could just do it," Pope says.

"We changed everything, six months before release."

In the process of putting these tools together, Pope rubbed elbows with a programming team that he effusively praises no less than four times in our chat. He came into Naughty Dog with a heavy focus on C#, only to find the team was working largely with languages he was rustier with, like C++ and Lisp. Pope credits team members like design lead and co-CEO Evan Wells with essentially inspiring him to operate with a higher level of confidence. Meaning, if they could wing it during that game's most intense production moments, and do great work, so could he.

"One of the cool things about Uncharted 1 is, we had no fucking idea what we were doing," Pope says. Again, he reminds me that the team was very capable, but design decisions were incredibly fluid at the last minute—including a total overhaul of the control system.

"Uncharted 1 was announced, and then Gears [of War] came out," Pope says. "And Gears invented the modern third-person shooter." Naughty Dog had originally begun its work on Uncharted with more of a Tomb Raider control system, Pope says, which meant more auto-rolling and auto-aiming. "Suddenly, Gears came out and showed them how to do it. So we changed everything, six months before release."

The short-story version of "everything" is a shift in camera perspective, controls, and general movement to incorporate more of an angled, third-person perspective, rather than using the behind-the-back standard of classic Lara Croft adventures. And the Naughty Dog dev team did this all while sticking with the studio's original promise of real-time cut scenes, no loading between sequences, and a fully voiced story.

When pressed about content that didn't make the cut for Uncharted 1, Pope offers a boilerplate answer of "mechanics and ideas" and avoids going into details. He says much of that eventually found its way into the second game. Then he recalls a series of real-time level sequences based in jungles that had been drawn and modeled by artists before level designers had converted that work into sequences for puzzles or combat. His jaw dropped at how incredible these sequences looked, running on PS3 hardware, but that sequence didn't fit the studio's vision for gameplay and got cut.

Pope concedes that big swaths of content get cut from games on a regular basis, "but to see the caliber of stuff that was cut, that was pretty insane."

His first and only sequel

Naughty Dog kept Pope on board for Uncharted 2 in the same role, where he refined existing tools and developed new ones. This was the first time in Pope's career that he hung around for a sequel, and in this case, there wasn't any question. His stint working on Uncharted 1 had flashed by too quickly, he thought, and he still wanted to rub elbows with Naughty Dog's prestigious team. And he had ideas for new GUI tools, like a "new, complicated shader tool" to let artists build shaders in the game's language, then translate them to Maya and review the results there.

"It was almost like a different project," Pope says. "And it wasn't my game. I didn't feel like I was having to, like, hit the same vein that I was hitting before."

"I [used to] make sure a bridge never collapses, even when Godzilla walks over it."

In the process of getting through two Uncharted games, Pope began accumulating the kind of experience he'd never considered relevant to being a good game maker: pragmatism. He credits Uncharted series tech lead Christophe Balestra, who ordered the staff to cut a wonky, hacked-together camera system that had been gumming up a game's code to some extent. "What if we just ditch the whole thing?" Pope recalls Balestra saying. "It's ugly, but the player will never see the code." Pope recalls "freaking out" when the game in question shipped and the game's hero "Nathan Drake looks, like, ridiculous [in certain instances]... but no one cared.

"It's a rite of passage, basically, where your whole mentality changes about what's important when you're working on a game," Pope says. He admits that experience helped him get over his "OCD engineer" perspective, where "we have to make sure a bridge never collapses, even when Godzilla walks over it."

Childhood dreams of wooden cows and robot lizards

Pope takes a break from his Naughty Dog stories once we approach a noodle shop. And as we take our time to place orders and slurp up meaty, fatty broth, the conversation turns to his childhood. Pope had mentioned working on cars with his dad; what was that like?

Pope recalls one of the first books he remembers getting from his father, "Handy Book For Boys, or something like that." He compared it to an "old Boy Scout manual, full of pen-and-ink drawings" of projects to be built from scratch. "Like a wooden cow you could make, and it would moo when its tail wags."

Thanks to his father's full-time job as a handyman, Pope enjoyed access to a decked-out garage. "From that age, I would look through the book and think, 'I want to build this with my dad,' and then we'd spend a couple of months sawing, hammering, painting--mechanically building a lot.'"

By the time he got to high school, Pope became friends with a kid who was interested in robots and computers, and this friendship fit neatly with Pope's childhood builder mentality. Almost immediately, Pope was convinced: "I want to make robots for a living." This interest began with tearing apart Radio Shack robots, then connecting them to Commodore 64 systems and controlling them through code. The controls were "nothing impressive," but he'd already begun dreaming big in the early '90s: "I wanted to make a miniature lizard that you could command to run around and sit on your hand."

"The magic is in software. There's no rules."

With that excitement in mind, Pope applied to Virginia Tech's mechanical engineer program. However, unlike the more straightforward building projects he'd done with his father and his high school buddy, he faced a harsh truth: "What I learned is that, uh, the real world is messy," Pope says. "And hard. You've got to deal with gearing and lubrication and switches and sensors, all the shit that I actually wasn't that interested in." He might have stuck with that portion of the engineering process... had he not felt that much more comfortable in programming classes. "Once I got into C++ programming, I realized, this is where I want to be. The magic happens in software. There's no rules."

Pope felt proficient. He'd already dabbled in Hypercard. He could apply his love of art ("I like to draw") and music (piano that he'd been forced to learn as a kid, and drumming he'd picked up in a high school speed-metal band called Aftermath) to computer-science projects. And he was starting to fall pretty hard for Quake, which launched in stores during the summer after his freshman year. (His work on that aforementioned Quake total-conversion mod, Malice, followed shortly after.)

